<h1>Maintenance &amp; Troubleshooting</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor preventive maintenance (PM) extends equipment life, prevents unplanned downtime, and maintains product quality on packaging lines. A structured PM program covers belt tracking, belt tensioning, roller and bearing inspection, lubrication, motor and drive checks, sensor verification, and safety device testing. Neglecting conveyor maintenance causes belt failures, motor burnouts, product contamination, and line stops that cost far more than the PM labor and parts they prevent.</p>
<h2 id="schedule-heading">Preventive Maintenance Schedule</h2>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/maint-dorner-drive-inspection.webp" width="1000" height="693" alt="Two hands reach around the end of a plastic chain conveyor where a toothed belt links a large pulley to a small sprocket on a black side plate." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Checking the toothed belt and pulleys at the end of a plastic chain conveyor. Hands-on checks of belts, drives, and guards at points like this make up most of a preventive maintenance round. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/blog/how-predictive-maintenance-reduces-conveyor-downtime" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Daily (Each Shift Start)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Visual belt inspection, check for cuts, tears, fraying, delamination, and product buildup.</li>
<li>Belt tracking check, observe the belt running and verify it stays centered. Correct minor drift immediately.</li>
<li>Listen for unusual sounds, grinding, squealing, clicking, or rhythmic thumping indicates bearing failure, belt splice issues, or roller problems.</li>
<li>Remove debris from the conveyor frame, belt edges, and underside. Product buildup causes tracking problems and bacterial growth in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food environments</a>.</li>
<li>Verify E-stop function, press each E-stop button and confirm the conveyor stops immediately. Release and confirm restart.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Weekly</h3>
<ul>
<li>Belt tension check, confirm the drive pulley moves the belt under full load without slipping and that the gauge marks (see <a href="#tension-heading">Belt Tensioning</a>) still show the belt maker's elongation. Adjust tension if necessary.</li>
<li>Roller spin test, manually spin each roller (on roller conveyors). Rollers should spin freely with no grinding, wobbling, or stiffness. Replace any roller that does not spin freely.</li>
<li>Sensor function test, block each photoeye and proximity sensor and verify the correct PLC response (conveyor stop, zone control, counting).</li>
<li>Guide rail alignment, verify guide rails are parallel to the belt and positioned at the correct width for the product. Misaligned guides cause jams and product damage.</li>
<li>Clean belt (food environments), remove the belt (if tool-free) and clean both surfaces. Clean the conveyor frame, pulleys, and slider bed while the belt is removed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Monthly</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bearing lubrication, grease bearings with the grease type, quantity, and interval the bearing maker specifies. Over-greasing forces grease into the belt contact area and contaminates product. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> use sealed bearings, no lubrication needed.</li>
<li>Motor temperature check, use an infrared thermometer to measure motor housing temperature. Compare to baseline. A motor running significantly hotter than baseline indicates overload, ventilation blockage, or bearing failure inside the motor.</li>
<li>Belt/chain wear measurement, measure belt thickness at multiple points and compare to new belt thickness. Replace the belt when the cover is worn through to the carcass or below the belt maker's minimum. For roller chain drives, measure pitch elongation: <a href="https://www.renold.com/media/1269222/chain-wear-guide-instructions.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Renold's chain wear guide</a> says to consider replacement at 2% elongation and replace immediately at 3%. For tabletop and other conveyor chain, use the chain maker's published limit.</li>
<li>Guard condition, inspect all safety guards and covers. Replace damaged guards immediately. Missing guards are serious hazards, and <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.212</a> requires guarding of ingoing nip points and rotating parts.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quarterly</h3>
<ul>
<li>Drive inspection, check gearbox oil level and condition (if applicable). Inspect drive belt or chain tension and alignment. Check sprocket and pulley wear (tooth profile should be symmetrical, not hooked).</li>
<li>Electrical connections, tighten all terminal connections in the conveyor junction box and motor connections. Vibration loosens connections over time, causing intermittent faults and arcing.</li>
<li>VFD parameter verification, compare VFD settings to the commissioning baseline. Unauthorized parameter changes cause speed errors and motor overheating.</li>
<li>Conveyor frame alignment, check frame straightness with a string line or laser. Frame deflection causes belt tracking problems that no amount of pulley adjustment can fix.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Annual</h3>
<ul>
<li>Full system inspection, every component from motor to tail pulley. Document condition with photos. Create a replacement plan for components approaching end-of-life.</li>
<li>Belt replacement assessment, evaluate whether the belt will last another year. Schedule proactive replacement during planned downtime rather than waiting for in-service failure.</li>
<li>Motor insulation test (megger test), measures motor winding insulation resistance. Low readings predict motor failure months before it occurs. Replace motors with declining insulation readings.</li>
<li>Controls system backup, back up PLC programs, HMI configurations, VFD parameters, and sensor settings. Store backups off-site. A PLC failure without a backup can take days to recover.</li>
<li>Safety system audit, verify all E-stops, guard interlocks, light curtains, and safety relays function correctly, and document the results. Separately, <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147(c)(6)</a> requires a periodic inspection of each lockout/tagout energy control procedure at least annually, with a written certification of who, what equipment, and when.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="tracking-heading">Belt Tracking</h2>
<p>Belt tracking is the most critical and most frequently needed conveyor maintenance task. A mistracking belt grinds against the conveyor frame, shredding the belt edge, contaminating product with belt material, and eventually destroying the belt. Mistracking also jams product, misaligns product for downstream equipment, and can trigger <a href="/conveyor-functions/inspection-detection/">inspection system</a> false rejects.</p>
<h3>Tracking Procedure</h3>
<p>The adjustment point and direction depend on the conveyor design, so follow the conveyor manual. Some designs track at a snub roller, some at the tail pulley take-up, and some at a cam. The method below follows the general guidance in <a href="https://www.habasit.com/-/media/Project/Habasit/NewWebsite/Downloads/Products/General-conveyor-belts/Fabric-Conveyor-Belts-Installation-and-Maintenance-Guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Habasit's fabric belt installation and maintenance guide</a> and the <a href="https://blog.hytrol.com/ask-hytrol-how-to-track-a-belt/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol belt tracking procedure</a>.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Run the belt empty.</strong> Remove all product. Run slowly or with frequent stops at first so a run-off tendency is caught before it damages the belt edge.</li>
<li><strong>Find where the drift starts.</strong> Note which direction the belt drifts and where it is worst (near the head, tail, or middle). Correct first where the belt is most at risk of damage.</li>
<li><strong>Make one small adjustment at one point.</strong> Adjust only one pulley or roller at a time. Adjusting several at once makes tracking unstable. On Hytrol belt conveyors, for example, you loosen the snub roller bolts nearest the tail pulley and tap the bolt on the side toward which the belt needs to move.</li>
<li><strong>Wait several revolutions.</strong> The belt needs a few turns to settle into the new alignment. Hytrol says the belt should begin moving toward center within seven revolutions.</li>
<li><strong>Repeat in small steps.</strong> Large adjustments overcorrect and create oscillating tracking problems. Hytrol's target is a belt within a quarter inch of the tail pulley center.</li>
<li><strong>Load product.</strong> After the belt tracks centered when empty, add product and verify tracking holds under load. Off-center loading pulls the belt toward the loaded side, so infeed alignment may need adjustment.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Tracking Does Not Respond to Adjustment</h3>
<p>If the belt does not respond to tail pulley adjustment, investigate these root causes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Material buildup on pulleys or rollers:</strong> Product, adhesive, or debris stuck to the drive pulley or return rollers creates a high spot that pushes the belt off-center. Clean all pulleys and rollers.</li>
<li><strong>Damaged or cupped belt:</strong> A belt that has taken a permanent curve (cupping) will not track straight regardless of adjustment. Replace the belt.</li>
<li><strong>Failed splice:</strong> A belt splice that is not straight or has begun separating pulls the belt off-track at the splice location. Re-splice or replace.</li>
<li><strong>Frame or pulleys out of square:</strong> The conveyor frame is not straight or the pulleys are not at right angles to the belt path. Habasit's troubleshooting checklist says to release the tension, square all pulleys and rollers, and re-tension before tracking again.</li>
<li><strong>Crowned pulley worn flat:</strong> Crowned (barrel-shaped) pulleys self-center the belt. If the crown wears flat, the belt loses its self-centering tendency. Replace the pulley.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="tension-heading">Belt Tensioning</h2>
<p>Correct belt tension provides enough friction between the belt and drive pulley to transmit motor power without the belt slipping, and no more. Over-tensioning stresses bearings, stretches the belt, increases motor load, and accelerates wear on the slider bed and belt bottom. Under-tensioning causes belt slip, which generates heat, glazes the drive pulley and belt underside, and eventually melts or burns the belt.</p>
<h3>Tension Check Method</h3>
<p>Fabric belt makers set tension by elongation, not by feel. <a href="https://www.habasit.com/-/media/Project/Habasit/NewWebsite/Downloads/Products/General-conveyor-belts/Fabric-Conveyor-Belts-Installation-and-Maintenance-Guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Habasit's installation guide</a> describes the method: put two marks a known distance apart on each side of the slack belt, tension until the marks have stretched by the required percentage, run the belt one full circuit, and measure again. Habasit lists the admissible initial elongation as 0.3% to about 1% for polyester-fabric belts and 0.5% to about 1.5% for polyamide-fabric belts, and its sizing software gives the exact figure for a given belt and conveyor.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Traction layer</th>
<th>Minimum initial elongation</th>
<th>Maximum initial elongation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Polyester fabric</td>
<td>0.3%</td>
<td>About 1%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Polyamide fabric</td>
<td>0.5%</td>
<td>About 1.5%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Source: Habasit, Fabric Conveyor Belts Installation and Maintenance Guide. Other belt makers publish their own figures, so use the values for the belt you have. Leave the marks on the belt. They make later tension checks and re-tensioning after maintenance quick and repeatable. Habasit warns that re-tensioning a released belt by the first-time method overtensions it, because the new elongation adds to the elongation that never fully released.</p>
<p>Belt manufacturers publish tension recommendations per <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">belt type</a> and width, and several offer belt calculation software that accounts for belt type, width, conveyor length, incline, and load. Use the maker's figure for setup and the gauge marks for ongoing checks.</p>
<h3>Adjusting Tension</h3>
<p>Tension adjusts by moving the tail pulley away from the head pulley (to increase tension) or toward the head pulley (to decrease tension). Most packaging conveyors use screw-type take-up bolts on both sides of the tail pulley. Turn both bolts equally to change tension without affecting tracking. Turning one side more than the other changes both tension and tracking simultaneously, a common mistake that creates two problems from one adjustment. Always adjust tension first, then track separately.</p>
<p><strong>New belt relaxation:</strong> Habasit notes that synthetic belts relax after first tensioning, so the effective tension drops over a running-in period of several hours before it levels off. Tension a new belt enough to stay slip-free after relaxation, check the gauge marks after the first circuit and again after the first shifts, and re-tension if needed without exceeding the maximum elongation.</p>
<h2 id="roller-heading">Roller &amp; Bearing Maintenance</h2>
<p>Rollers and bearings are the highest-wear components in <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyors</a>, and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>. A failed roller creates a dead spot that jams product, damages the belt (in belt-driven systems), and can contaminate product if bearing grease or metal fragments enter the product stream.</p>
<h3>Roller Spin Test</h3>
<p>The roller spin test is the simplest and most effective roller check. With the conveyor stopped and locked out, manually spin each roller. A healthy roller spins freely and coasts to a smooth stop. Compare against a known-good roller of the same type. Replace any roller that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Does not spin at all (seized bearing).</li>
<li>Spins with grinding or crunching sound (bearing failure in progress).</li>
<li>Wobbles visibly (bent shaft or loose bearing).</li>
<li>Has a worn, grooved, or damaged surface (causes belt tracking issues and product damage).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bearing Lubrication</h3>
<p>Pillow block and flange bearings on conveyor drive shafts, tail pulleys, and idler rollers require periodic grease lubrication. Use the grease type, quantity, and interval specified by the bearing manufacturer. General-purpose NLGI 2 greases are common on conveyor bearings, and <a href="https://cdn.skfmediahub.skf.com/api/public/0901d196802103bc/pdf_preview_medium/0901d196802103bc_pdf_preview_medium.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SKF's grease selection chart</a> lists conveyors as an application for its general-purpose LGMT 2 grade. For <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">food-grade applications</a>, use NSF H1-registered grease.</p>
<p>SKF states on the same chart that poor lubrication accounts for over 36% of premature bearing failures. <strong>Over-greasing is also a common and costly mistake.</strong> Excess grease forces past the bearing seal, contaminating the belt and product. In food environments, grease contamination causes product rejects, customer complaints, and potential recalls. Apply the manufacturer-specified quantity, no more.</p>
<p>MDR conveyors and many modern roller conveyors use sealed-for-life bearings that require no lubrication. This eliminates the over-greasing risk and reduces PM labor. When specifying new conveyors, sealed bearings reduce long-term maintenance cost and contamination risk.</p>
<h2 id="trouble-heading">Common Problems &amp; Solutions</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Problem</th>
<th>Likely Causes</th>
<th>Corrective Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Belt drifts to one side</td>
<td>Tail pulley misalignment, material buildup on pulley, frame not straight, damaged belt</td>
<td>Make a small tracking adjustment. Clean pulleys. Check frame alignment. Inspect belt for damage.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Belt slips on drive pulley</td>
<td>Insufficient tension, wet/contaminated pulley, worn lagging, overloaded belt</td>
<td>Increase tension. Clean drive pulley. Replace lagging. Reduce product load or increase motor HP.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Squealing noise</td>
<td>Belt slipping on drive pulley, dry bearing, belt rubbing frame</td>
<td>Increase tension (if slipping). Lubricate bearing. Correct tracking (if rubbing).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Grinding or clicking</td>
<td>Bearing failure, roller damage, chain link issue, foreign object</td>
<td>Identify the specific roller/bearing/chain link. Replace damaged component. Remove foreign object.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Motor trips on overload</td>
<td>Belt too tight, seized roller/bearing, product jam, undersized motor</td>
<td>Reduce belt tension. Spin-test all rollers. Clear jam. Verify motor HP matches load requirement.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Motor overheating</td>
<td>Blocked ventilation, overload, high ambient temperature, VFD low-speed operation</td>
<td>Clear ventilation. Check load vs. motor rating. On constant-torque loads run at low speed, confirm the motor is rated for that turndown or add a separate cooling blower.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product jams at transfer point</td>
<td>Speed mismatch, gap too wide, guide rail misalignment, product orientation</td>
<td>Match conveyor speeds. Reduce dead plate gap. Align guides. Check upstream product orientation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Belt edge fraying</td>
<td>Belt rubbing frame, mistracking, guide rail contact, belt too wide</td>
<td>Correct tracking. Adjust guide rail clearance. Verify belt width matches conveyor width specification.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="motor-heading">Motor &amp; Drive Maintenance</h2>
<p>Conveyor motors fail from three causes: overheating (insulation degradation), bearing failure (lubrication or contamination), and electrical failure (winding shorts, connection faults). A structured motor maintenance program addresses all three.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Temperature monitoring:</strong> Record motor housing temperature monthly with an infrared thermometer. Establish a baseline during commissioning. A clear, sustained rise above baseline warrants investigation: check ventilation, load, ambient temperature, and VFD parameters. Heat is what ages winding insulation. As <a href="https://www.ecmweb.com/content/article/20899136/the-hot-issue-of-motor-temperature-ratings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EC&amp;M reports</a>, insulation life drops by half for each 10°C (18°F) of operation above the insulation's rated temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Motor bearing lubrication:</strong> Motor bearings that have grease fittings require regreasing on a schedule set by the motor maker, based on frame size, speed, and duty. Over-greasing motor bearings forces grease into the motor windings, causing insulation failure. Follow the motor manufacturer's grease type and quantity specifications exactly.</li>
<li><strong>VFD maintenance:</strong> Keep VFD cooling fans clean and air filters clear. Dust accumulation causes VFD overheating and premature capacitor failure. DC bus capacitors age with heat and time, so follow the drive maker's inspection and replacement guidance, especially on older drives. Back up all VFD parameters after commissioning and after any changes.</li>
<li><strong>Gearbox oil:</strong> Gear reducers require regular oil level checks and oil changes on the reducer maker's schedule, which depends on oil type, operating temperature, and hours. Use the specified oil type and viscosity. Contaminated or low oil levels destroy gear teeth and bearings. Synthetic gear oils extend change intervals and improve efficiency in cold environments.</li>
</ul>
<p>For conveyor motor and drive cost factors, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>. For <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>, PLC and controls maintenance adds another layer, program backups, I/O testing, and network diagnostics.</p>
<h2 id="spares-heading">Recommended Spare Parts</h2>
<p>Stocking the right spare parts prevents extended downtime when a component fails. Compare the cost of carrying each spare against the production lost while waiting for an emergency shipment. Recommended spares for packaging conveyor systems:</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/maint-dorner-timing-belt.webp" width="1000" height="562" alt="Two hands thread a green toothed belt around a drive sprocket and two idlers under an aluminum conveyor frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Threading a toothed belt through the drive under a conveyor frame. With a spare belt in stock, this job can wait for a planned stop. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/blog/how-predictive-maintenance-reduces-conveyor-downtime" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<ul>
<li><strong>Belts:</strong> One spare belt per conveyor type and size. For <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">modular belts</a>, stock replacement modules and hinge rods rather than complete belts.</li>
<li><strong>Rollers:</strong> Stock spares based on how many fail per year on your lines, matching diameter, length, and bearing type. For MDR conveyors, stock motorized rollers and slave rollers separately.</li>
<li><strong>Bearings:</strong> Stock pillow block and flange bearings matching every shaft size in the system. Bearings are commodity items with long shelf life.</li>
<li><strong>Motors:</strong> Stock one spare motor per motor size used in the system. Non-standard ratings, washdown motors, and gearmotors can have long lead times. A spare on the shelf turns a long wait into a same-shift repair.</li>
<li><strong>Sensors:</strong> Stock spare photoeyes, proximity switches, and encoders, one per type used. Sensors are exposed to impacts and cleaning chemicals and are quick to swap.</li>
<li><strong>Drive components:</strong> Spare drive belts, chains, sprockets, and pulleys. One set per conveyor drive type.</li>
<li><strong>Pneumatic components:</strong> Spare solenoid valves, air cylinders (for <a href="/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/">pushers and diverters</a>), and filter/regulator elements.</li>
</ul>
